title: Detecting Edges
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex05_Button1P.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex06_Button2P.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/EdgeI.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/GpioI.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-09-01.png
  - .assets/img-101-09-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp; Edge Detection}

      This tour visits two programs which wink `AppLed` &ndash; but now in response to asynchrnously pressing `AppBut`:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]Button1P}, which uses a primitive _super-loop_<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]Button2P}, which relies upon _fiber dispatch_

      {[in]An earlier <b>One-Shot Timers</b> tour took a similar ap&shy;proach &ndash; contrasting two similar program designs. }

      Looking at the {[cu]Button1P} signal capture, you can press `AppBut` at any point in time and for any duration.

      Here, {[cu]Button1P} winks `AppLed` at the _beginning_ of the button press &ndash; when `AppBut` transitions 1&thinsp;&rarr;&thinsp;0.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 7
      - mark 1 B 12
      - mark 1 C 20
      - mark 1 D 28
      - mark 1 E 37
    focus: [1, 11]
    acts: [$reveal, $build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp; %[cu]Button1P% &ndash; Details}

      Like the {[cu]OneShot1P} program seen prior, {[cu]Button1P} main&shy;tains its portability by using an {[cu]AppButEdge} {[cs]$delegate}:

      {[BM,A]} We access `AppBut` via the current {[cu]BoardC} <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>.<br>
      {[BM,B]} We bind a detection handler at build-time [**`meta`**].<br>
      {[BM,C]} We setup the `AppBut` pin HW at run-time [**`targ`**].<br>
      {[BM,D]} We enable `AppBut` detection inside our super-loop.<br>
      {[BM,E]} We clear dection before signaling {[cn]pressed_flag}.

      {[in]The <b>EMS</b> translator ensures all %[cs]em$startup% code exe&shy;cutes <i>before</i> the target C++ program reaches %[cs]em$run%.}

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 10
      - mark 2 B 15
      - mark 2 C 26
      - mark 2 D 29
      - mark 2 E 38
    focus: [2, 8]
    acts: [$reveal, $build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp; %[cu]Button2P% &ndash; Details}

      In contrast with {[cu]Button1P}, the {[cu]Button2P} program uses {[cu]FiberMgr} in a conventional manner:

      {[BM,A]} We declare a {[cs]$config} which holds a {[ct]Fiber} object.<br>
      {[BM,B]} We bind a new {[ct]Fiber} and its callback at build-time.<br>
      {[BM,C]} We start dispatching {[ct]Fiber} objects at run-time.<br>
      {[BM,D]} {[cf]blinkFB} winks `AppLed` and re-enables `AppBut`.<br>
      {[BM,E]} {[cf]handler} invokes {[cf]post} to schedule {[cf]blinkFB}.

      {[in]Here again, prefer <i>fiber dispatch</i> over an event <i>super-loop</i> when designing your application code.}

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |

      {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp; %[cu]Button2P% &ndash; Signal Capture}

      Using {[cu]Button2P}, we'll now zoom-in on a single `AppLed` wink triggered by the falling edge of `AppBut`.

      For orientation, labels {[dc,C]} and {[dc,D]} mark execution of {[cd]%%c} and {[cd]%%d} inside {[cf]handler} and {[cf]blinkFB}.

      The pulses on `DbgB` track program entry into _lite-sleep_ (after {[cf]wink}) versus _deep-sleep_ (after {[cf]enableDetect}).

      {[in]Unlike timer events, we see the <i>exact</i> point where `AppBut` falls and can precisely measure latency.}

  - cmds:
      - open 5
      - mark 5 A 27
      - mark 5 B 28
      - mark 5 C 60
      - mark 5 D 61
    focus: [5, 31]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp; %[cu]BoardC% &ndash; Details}

      The {[cu]BoardC} **composite** instaniates {[cu]AppButEdge}{[bm,A]} and {[cu]AppButPin}{[bm,B]} using {[cs]$clone} with a **template**.

      {[in]The next tour will add %[cu]AppBut% to the mix.}

      {[cu]AppButEdge} itself exposes a {[cn]Pin} **proxy** &ndash; here bound {[bm,C]}to {[cu]AppButPin} for its **delegate**.

      {[cu]AppButEdge} and {[cu]AppButPin} assign {[bm,D]} their {[cn]pin_num} **config** parameters a board-specific value for `AppBut`

      The {[cu]AppButEdge} and {[cu]AppButPin} **delegates** repsectively implement {[cu]EdgeI} and {[cu]GpioI} **interfaces** &ndash; seen next.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
    focus: [3, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp; %[cu]EdgeI% &ndash; Quick Look}

      {[cu]Button1P} and {[cu]Button2P} each interact with {[cu]AppButEdge} using meth&shy;ods specified in the {[cu]EdgeI} **interface**.

      Unlike most {[cu]EdgeI} methods, clients can only invoke {[cf]setDetectHandler} at _build-time_ [**`meta`**].

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp; %[cu]GpioI% &ndash; Quick Look}

      {[cu]GpioI} provides a "low-level" HW **interface** often hid&shy;den beneath other abstractions &ndash; {[cu]EdgeI}, {[cu]LedI}, etc.

      We first saw this **interface** within a **proxy** declared in&shy;side the (portable) {[cs]'@em.utils/SoftUart.em'} **module**.

      Unlike other {[cu]GpioI} methods, note that clients can call {[cf]pinId} at _build-time_ [**`meta`**] as well as _run-time_ [**`targ`**].

      {[in]In general, any <b>unit</b> can expose a common suite of getter&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;setter methods for <i>both</i> <b>EMS</b> domains.}

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Measure HW&thinsp;&rarr;&thinsp;SW detection latency in **PulseView**.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Capture programs using {[cf]setDetectFalling} instead.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Explore board-specific implementations of {[cu]EdgeI}.

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      Almost done with the **Basic Examples** tour bundle&thinsp;&hellip;

      Its final tour introduces a higher-level {[cu]ButtonI} **inter&shy;face** that leverages abstractions you've already studied.

      {[hc]}
